#{[17592186087934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087934 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087934 #edb/ref 1045 "Dilations and Reflections as Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087934 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087934 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087934 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bc935836-c5f6-5bf6-8105-6072d1f0946d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1045 "Parametric Equations of Parabolas" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "388f5ace-d291-564e-ba4f-c8bd5e29bb8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088430 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,-3)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bfc19725-defa-5a63-87f9-f8a8fdd626b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088430 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3=2pt \\\\ -3=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088514 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088514 #edb/ref 1061 16069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088514 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22697b73-625d-545a-ba00-223291b29933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088514 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088515 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088515 #edb/ref 1061 16070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088515 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b1b7d3eb-f611-589c-99d3-92b8c28fd2c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088515 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088516 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/r