186045417] [17592186093060 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "68d9dc46-2592-5260-a1bb-53e6b885e53f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093060 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093061 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093061 #edb/ref 1061 20047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093061 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9915017c-1452-5136-bb94-65860a727a47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093061 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093062 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093062 #edb/ref 1061 20053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093062 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d48d44d1-fe67-587c-a56e-251eee6b68f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093062 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093135 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093135 #edb/ref 1061 26469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093135 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "496d3d86-9264-56e9-a8a9-63d5cf7428bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093135 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093260 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093260 #edb/ref 1061 464465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093260 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b310d56-7a89-527c-bf59-b2f90684f0c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093260 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093261 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093261 #edb/ref 1061 464466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093261 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96b175ec-ed88-5e8d-8030-67a565348dd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093261 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093262 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093262 #edb/ref 1061 464467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093262 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6c8ba1d-d40a-5c09-b23f-464fd88b700f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093262 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093263 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093263 #edb/ref 1061 464470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093263 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c949ec17-7c15-564d-a513-e970b0226b29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093263 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093264 #edb/ref 1061 464474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093264 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c3d8379-0f57-5843-bba4-5e8fe0677978" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093264 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093402 #edb/ref 1064 "If the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nby \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ {I}_{2}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}=A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor instance, to find the solution to the system of equations\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n we first calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the solution to this system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solution is $x=3$ and $y=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093402 #edb/ref 1065 3049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093402 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "39b17ee8-086a-5282-a414-c88d455b6107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093402 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093403 #edb/ref 1064 "We know that we can write a system of three linear equations in three variables in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n- $A$ is the $3\\times 3$ coefficient matrix,\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}$ is the column vector of variables, and\n\n- $\\mathbf{b}$ is the column vector of constants.\n\nIf the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ by \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ {I}_{3}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nLet's see how this works for a particular system in the next example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093403 #edb/ref 1065 3054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093403 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4c0fe954-2cf5-5eb6-b58f-c6963f5be7f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093403 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093404 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=3 \\\\ 6x+3y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we use the matrix method of $\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}$ to find a solution to this system?\n\nThis system can be represented in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo use this matrix method, we need to find $A^{-1}.$ However\n\n$$\n\\det (A) = 2\\cdot 3 - 1\\cdot 6 =0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is not invertible. So we cannot use the matrix method to find a solution to the system.\n\nIn general, if the coefficient matrix $A$ is not invertible, there is no unique solution. This means that either\n\n- a solution may not exist, or\n\n- there may be infinitely many solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093404 #edb/ref 1065 3066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093404 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e5e2bff7-df5b-5628-92e3-a50ca8dd6be8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093404 #edb/ref 1067 "Systems of Equations Where the Coefficient Matrix is Singular" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093431 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider once again the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square that is spanned by these vectors.\n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/8f/8fca8cf6dd5b232d3aed6eeb98fb6c244872c55425673cf3ac1441a22e2e29f3.svg) \n\nAs before, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/3f/3f8b5f0f827d359f32a4311021dc48947447133918d48a2f1cad511531b13c81.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/41/4195c8cd2f22fb0f84056226fb7b28c5f9944f747ca3230bf187752829b2a04e.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, *clockwise* about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093431 #edb/ref 1065 3542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093431 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "70a75b2e-3fa9-5aa9-b308-79b56d57fae7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093431 #edb/ref 1067 "Rotations of 90 Degrees" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093432 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/c6/c6865c5373231451885b3d76e3c8151e9c707462a8b405d9b014b1bda17065a8.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by computing the matrix products, as follows:\n![](images/5b/5b8e1c8e03ebb60716eaa2fefded5e7fc286f6de9cffdece8200f6f56c2bbe8e.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c2dc9f76bb23225cec0a4f62541d8a74c4c4b16d2e2f7c37e8d36922d97c59f0.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) about the origin.**\n\nNote that we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) *clockwise* about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093432 #edb/ref 1065 3545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093432 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "410fc9b7-ab2d-52ba-9b26-7afd34fdf9e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093432 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093433 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation $\\mathbf R$ by its standard matrix $R,$ given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{R}$ by computing the matrix products, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =R(i)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {j}^{'} & =R(j)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlotting the vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ and their images $\\mathbf i'$ and $\\mathbf j',$ we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/35/3516ba56a842adb78f1acae9190b65eb1574629e9f3d6bca25854f547f69385f.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation $\\mathbf R$ represented by the matrix $R$ defines a **counterclockwise rotation of $30^\\circ$ about the origin.**\n\nAs usual, we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation $\\mathbf R$ can also be considered a rotation of $360^\\circ-30^\\circ=330^\\circ$ *clockwise* about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093433 #edb/ref 1065 3546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093433 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "427eca20-6aad-5c52-a545-fc66d808e5b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093433 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093434 #edb/ref 1064 "Finally, let \n\n![](images/1b/1b840cbdb5b3953dfef9910d34248ea280bf38299e4f0a3d0a78143e7301f159.svg)\n\nbe a matrix of a linear transformation ![](images/2d/2d90be63023ff97e21e1a97ccee6c387437ac2a99787278c6f71f37e3ac86850.svg) As usual, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/76/76e24611a2cd8a3d5d01fc0a04a9b7a3d0957db84dd1a45bc2f1f82492fb2efd.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0f/0fc5f33d0bd7047ab02bd9bc3ea3b00c083ab214f3bc8a51e9cbb6e54dc6b51b.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians *clockwise* about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093434 #edb/ref 1065 3547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093434 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "49541094-17cb-52e7-bb58-8ee2199ad6d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093434 #edb/ref 1067 "Rotations of 270 Degrees" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093435 #edb/ref 1064 "The matrix $R_\\theta,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nrepresents a **rotation by an angle $\\theta$ counterclockwise about the origin.**\n\nTo find the matrix representing a rotation by an angle $\\theta$ *clockwise* about the origin, we replace $\\theta$ with